description:
Static map of a Python repository. Schema 1.3.0 is the frozen RepoMap v1 contract for Translate. Limitations are recorded in schema/v1-contract.md. New exports are versioned documents (schema_version 1.3.0). A bare JSON array of file records is still accepted.

Version 1.3.0 records entry points on each file. A __main__ block is an entry, including the calls it makes. A module-level function or class is an entry when it belongs to that module's public surface or is listed in __all__. A package __init__.py also contributes the functions and classes it re-exports. An uncalled private helper is not an entry point. Version 1.2.0 adds language-neutral types for a later Java or C++ translation. Annotations are extracted into that type language. Literals, assignments, constructor calls, and the method receiver fill in types the source does not annotate. An async function's inferred return type is the value it returns, matching its annotation, not the coroutine wrapper. Version 1.1.0 stores a signature and a body on every function and method. The signature has parameters (kind, annotation, and default), type parameters, and the return annotation. The body lists statements in source order: control flow (if, for, while, try, and the with and match blocks that contain them), return, raise, assignments, and expression statements. Expressions keep literals and the operators, calls, and names around them. A nested function or class is only a marker in the enclosing body; its statements live on its own function detail. elif is an if statement in orelse.

Paths are repository-relative and use forward slashes. The src/ directory prefix is kept in paths and ids; it is stripped only when deriving a Python module name for import resolution.

Directories named .git, .venv, __pycache__, and node_modules are skipped. Files that fail to parse, are not UTF-8, contain null bytes, overflow the parser, or resolve outside the repository are omitted.

Stable ids identify a file, class, method, or function inside one map. They stay the same across runs when the relative path and definition name stay the same. A line number is appended when a file has more than one top-level definition with that name, when a method id would otherwise be shared by two methods, and always for definitions nested inside a function. Those line-qualified ids change if the definition moves to a different line.

Id grammar:
- file:{path}
- class:{path}:{name} or class:{path}:{name}:{line}
- method:{path}:{class}:{name} or method:{path}:{class}:{name}:{line}
- function:{path}:{name} or function:{path}:{name}:{line}

Nested classes and functions always use the line-qualified form. Nested methods use method:{path}:{class}:{name}:{line}.
